.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SET_THREAD_AREA 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm set_thread_area ,
.Nm get_thread_area ,
.Nm set_tid_address
.Nd set the thread's TLS base and its end's futex word
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.In unistd.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "long number" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Musl declares no public function for either call.
Use
.Fn syscall
with
.Dv SYS_set_thread_area
(i386 number 243) or
.Dv SYS_set_tid_address
(258).
Each call takes one pointer argument in EBX.
Musl's internal
.Fn __set_thread_area
issues number 243; its
.Fn __init_tp
initialization routine issues number 258.
.Pp
.Nm set_thread_area
sets the base of the calling thread's thread-local storage (TLS).
The argument points to the following 16-byte kernel ABI structure;
each field is a 32-bit word:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct user_desc {
        unsigned int entry_number;
        unsigned int base_addr;
        unsigned int limit;
        unsigned int flags;
};
.Ed
.Pp
The kernel provides one TLS descriptor, entry 6 in the global descriptor
table (GDT).
.Va entry_number
must be 0xffffffff to request allocation, or 6 to replace that entry.
Both requests select the same entry.
.Va base_addr
is stored in the process record and applied to the GDT descriptor.
The base is reapplied on every context switch.
.Pp
The
.Va flags
word must request a present, writable, 32-bit data segment:
bit 0 must be set, bits 1 and 2 must be zero, bit 3 must be zero, and
bit 5 must be zero.
The limit-in-pages bit (4), useable bit (6), remaining flag bits, and
.Va limit
are ignored.
The descriptor retains its full 4 GiB limit.
Musl supplies flags 0x51 and limit 0xfffff.
.Pp
The call writes 6 back to
.Va entry_number .
The caller can then load
.Ql "(entry_number << 3) | 3"
(0x33) into %gs.
If this writeback fails, the new TLS base has already been installed.
.Pp
.Nm set_tid_address
records its pointer argument as the address the caller's thread end
clears, replacing any earlier one, and returns the caller's thread ID.
When the thread ends, and when it calls
.Xr execve 2 ,
the kernel writes 0 there and wakes one
.Xr futex 2
waiter on the word, if another thread shares the address space; this is
how
.Fn pthread_join
learns of a thread's end.
.Xr execve 2
forgets the address, and a child made by
.Xr fork 2
starts without one unless
.Xr clone 2
names one with
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID .
.Pp
On the x86-64 kernel a 64-bit thread's TLS base is the FS base, which
.Xr arch_prctl 2
sets and reads, and which
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
gives a new thread; its
.Ql syscall
table registers no
.Nm set_thread_area .
.Nm set_tid_address
is call 218 there, with the address in RDI, and does what it does on
i386.
.Pp
32-bit code reaches
.Nm set_thread_area
(243) and
.Nm get_thread_area
(244) on the x86-64 kernel by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
through the compat table of
.Xr ia32 7 ,
with the pointer in EBX.
There a thread has three TLS entries, 12, 13 and 14 of the GDT, as on
Linux.
.Va entry_number
0xffffffff takes the lowest one whose descriptor is empty and writes its
number back; 12 to 14 replace that entry; any other number fails with
.Er EINVAL .
The flags are checked as Linux checks them: the segment must be present
and 32-bit, its contents at most 1 (an expand-down data segment), and a
descriptor of
.Va base_addr ,
.Va limit
and
.Va flags
all zero, or of the read-exec-only and not-present bits alone, empties the
entry.
The base, limit, limit-in-pages, read-exec-only and useable bits are kept.
The selector is
.Ql "(entry_number << 3) | 3" :
0x63, 0x6b or 0x73.
A data segment register of the calling thread that holds the changed
selector is loaded again, with the null selector when the entry was
emptied.
The three descriptors are part of the thread's state: the kernel writes
them into the processor's GDT on every switch to the thread, a child made
by
.Xr fork 2
or
.Xr clone 2
copies its parent's, and
.Xr execve 2
empties them.
An i386
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
on x86-64 replaces the requested descriptor in the child.
If the child's FS or GS selector names that entry, its saved base changes
too, so the first context switch uses the child's thread pointer.
.Pp
.Nm get_thread_area
reads the entry whose number the
.Vt user_desc
at its argument names, from 12 to 14, and fills the structure with the
entry's base, limit and flags.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Nm set_thread_area
and
.Nm get_thread_area
return zero on success.
.Nm set_tid_address
returns the calling thread's ID.
The raw calls return a negative error number on failure;
.Fn syscall
instead returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/tlsprobe.c
requests entry 0xffffffff with flags 0x51 and a base pointing to its
thread control block, then loads the returned selector into %gs.
It checks stored values across system calls, timer interrupts, and
context switches, including a child with a different TLS base.
On x86-64,
.Pa tests/wow64probe.c
takes an entry by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
reads its block through selector 0x63 from 32-bit and 64-bit code, before
and after a sleep, and reads the entry back with
.Nm get_thread_area .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Nm set_thread_area
cannot read the descriptor or write back its entry number, or
.Nm get_thread_area
cannot read or write the structure.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The requested entry number or segment flags are not supported.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process, or on x86-64
.Nm set_thread_area
was asked for a free entry and all three are in use.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arch_prctl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr getpid 2 ,
.Xr ia32 7
